"executive_summary": "Topological valley photonics exploits the valley degree of freedom to control electromagnetic waves, but prior work has been confined to two dimensions, where photonic confinement along the third dimension is usually achieved in a non-topological way such as total internal reflection. Such 2D systems support single-polarization valley states, suffer from out-of-plane losses, and can only manipulate light flow in planar geometries. Full confinement and full control of light flow require a complete photonic bandgap, in which light of any polarization is forbidden to propagate in any direction in three dimensions, and this remains a significant challenge. This paper introduces a theoretical and experimental framework of three-dimensional (3D) topological valley photonics with a complete photonic bandgap and vectorial valley-contrasting physics. The 3D valley systems exhibit triple pairs of valleys characterized by valley Chern vectors, enabling vectorial bulk valley vortices and canalized chiral valley surface states. The valley Chern vectors and the circulating propagation direction of the valley surface states are intrinsically governed by the right-hand-thumb rule. The authors use a 3D photonic crystal with lattice constant a = 20 mm, built from perfect electric conductor spheres and rods, and break inversion symmetry by setting D1 = 6.4 mm and D2 = 3.6 mm to open a complete bandgap. They verify vectorial valley-contrasting Berry curvature using a vectorial chiral source array and pump-probe measurements, and demonstrate robust canalized valley surface states on all surfaces of a shaped sample. The measured outcoupling directions at 13.8 GHz are −32.9°, 0°, and +32.9°, corresponding to Wy−, X, and Wy+. The surface states propagate diffractionlessly along +y corresponding to the Wx− valley, and the right-hand-thumb rule is confirmed across (001) and (010) surfaces. The findings highlight applications in 3D waveguiding, directional radiation, and imaging, and suggest extension to terahertz and optical ranges.",
